---
/**
 * Theming and design tokens (ADR-002).
 *
 * The quick-start lists this guide because theming is the first customization
 * most consumers attempt. The content is the token decision: semantic tokens
 * with fallbacks, two themes, one override surface — and what is already
 * guaranteed (contrast gates, forced colors, reduced motion) so a theme change
 * does not have to re-earn those.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout title="Theming and design tokens" description="How Santaka UI themes work: token tiers, CSS custom property overrides, light/dark, and what is already guaranteed.">
  <h1>Theming and design tokens</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A component never hardcodes a color. It reads a semantic token with a literal
    fallback — <code>var(--sk-color-primary, #6d28d9)</code> — so theming is
    <em>overriding variables on a scope</em>, not patching component CSS. The
    fallback is the reason a component still renders correctly before any theme
    stylesheet loads; once the theme is imported, the token wins everywhere.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The three tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Tokens are authored in DTCG JSON under <code>packages/tokens/src</code> and
      compiled by the pipeline described in ADR-002 (a hand-rolled Node build, no
      style-dictionary, because the build must fail loudly on an unknown
      reference or a failed contrast gate before writing anything).
    </p>
    <pre><code>color.primitives.json     primitives: `color.&lt;ramp&gt;.&lt;step&gt;`, e.g. `color.ink.800`
dimensions.tokens.json    space, radius, border, shadow, motion, glass, type
themes/light.tokens.json  semantic roles: `color.primary`, `color.text`, …
themes/dark.tokens.json   the same roles, dark values</code></pre>
    <p>
      Primitives are internal: the build deletes them from the resolver output,
      so <code>color.ink.800</code> never ships as a CSS variable. The semantic
      roles are the published contract — <code>--sk-color-text</code>,
      <code>--sk-color-primary</code>, <code>--sk-color-border-strong</code>, and
      so on. Components consume semantic tokens plus the theme-independent
      dimensions (<code>--sk-space-4</code>, <code>--sk-border-thick</code>,
      <code>--sk-duration-control</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Override the semantic tier.</strong> Redefining a primitive
      variable changes nothing (they are not emitted); restating component CSS
      is exactly the maintenance this design removes. One redeclaration of
      <code>--sk-color-primary</code> re-fills the primary button, the focus
      ring text, and every other consumer of that role.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why components carry fallbacks</h2>
    <p>
      In <code>sk-button.css</code> the host block maps semantic tokens onto
      component-scoped variables:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sk-button &#123;
  --sk-button-bg: var(--sk-color-primary, #6d28d9);
  --sk-button-border: var(--sk-color-border-strong, #8b85a0);
  --sk-button-shadow: var(--sk-shadow-brutal, 5px 5px 0 0);
  ...
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Each <code>var()</code> carries the token value as a literal fallback
      (section 4 of the plan requires every semantic token to document its
      fallback). If a theme stylesheet is missing or renamed, a component does
      not throw — it quietly renders the fallback, which is slightly wrong but
      never broken. This is also why a token <em>rename</em> fails silently, and
      why the e2e suite asserts computed values rather than trusting CSS files.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to theme</h2>
    <p>
      Redeclare a <code>--sk-*</code> variable after the token stylesheets, at
      the scope you want it to apply:
    </p>
    <pre><code>:root &#123;
  --sk-color-primary: oklch(0.62 0.19 260);
&#125;

/* or scoped to a subtree */
[data-sk-theme="dark"] aside &#123;
  --sk-color-surface: oklch(0.2 0.02 280);
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Specificity does the rest: a component resolves
      <code>var(--sk-color-primary)</code> at runtime from its nearest declaring
      ancestor, so a root override themes the app and a subtree override themes
      one panel. No build step, no theme object, no JavaScript.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Light and dark</h2>
    <p>
      The build emits one stylesheet per theme:
      <code>light.css</code> under <code>:root</code> and <code>dark.css</code>
      under <code>[data-sk-theme="dark"]</code>. Dark is selected by attribute,
      so it works with server rendering and without JavaScript — set
      <code>data-sk-theme="dark"</code> on any ancestor. Direction follows
      <code>dir="rtl"</code> the same way; there is no RTL build and no second
      stylesheet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run <code>theme init</code> (<code>santaka</code> CLI,
      <code>packages/cli/src/commands.ts</code>, <code>themeInit</code>) and it
      writes <code>santaka-theme.css</code> into your project root. The file
      imports both theme stylesheets (<code>@santaka-ui/tokens/css/light.css</code>
      and <code>dark.css</code>) and carries the override contract as comments:
      the dark attribute, the <code>:root</code> override example, and the RTL
      note. It is written once, after the token and component stylesheets, and
      is the file you are expected to edit — not saved from anywhere. Running it
      again is idempotent: if the file already matches, it reports "already up to
      date".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Contrast is a gate, not a hope</h2>
    <p>
      The token build runs a contrast gate before it writes anything (<code>
      CONTRAST_GATES</code> in <code>packages/tokens/scripts/build-tokens.mjs</code>):
      14 pair checks per theme, 27 measured pairs total (the shadow gate is
      light-only, since a dark-theme shadow is gated through
      <code>color.border-strong</code>). Text pairs are held to 4.5:1, surfaces
      and indicators to 3:1. Any failure stops the build —
      <code>dist/css/contrast-report.txt</code> records every measured ratio,
      e.g. <code>color.text</code> at 18.21:1 (light) and 17.54:1 (dark),
      <code>color.text-muted</code> at 6.64:1 and 7.96:1.
    </p>
    <p>
      The e2e suite asserts the shipped colors, not just the build. <code>
      materials.e2e.ts</code> verifies glass is a real material — nonzero radius,
      a parsed <code>blur()</code> radius of 24px, background alpha at least 0.7
      so text stays readable even where backdrop-filter degrades — and that it
      differs across themes. <code>text.e2e.ts</code> asserts muted text resolves
      to a real, non-transparent color distinct from primary text: the 4.5:1
      gate lives in the build, and the test confirms the gated color actually
      arrives. When you override a semantic token, you inherit the plumbing but
      not the gate — keep <code>color.text</code>-on-<code>color.surface</code>
      at 4.5:1 or better.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What components already handle</h2>
    <p>
      A theme change does not have to re-earn accessibility behaviors the
      components carry:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Reduced motion:</strong> 13 component stylesheets under
        <code>packages/web-components/src/components/*/&lt;component&gt;.css</code>
        (<code>sk-accordion</code>, <code>sk-button</code>,
        <code>sk-checkbox</code>, <code>sk-icon-button</code>,
        <code>sk-input</code>, <code>sk-link</code>, <code>sk-progress</code>,
        <code>sk-radio-group</code>, <code>sk-select</code>,
        <code>sk-skeleton</code>, <code>sk-spinner</code>,
        <code>sk-switch</code>, <code>sk-textarea</code>) carry
        <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</code> blocks. They remove
        the movement — <code>translate: none</code>, <code>animation: none</code>,
        <code>transition: none</code> — rather than shortening it, because a 1ms
        slide is still a slide.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forced colors:</strong> 22 component stylesheets carry
        <code>@media (forced-colors: active)</code> overrides
        (<code>sk-separator</code> is the one exception it documents itself: a
        Canvas-colored hairline is already the forced palette's intent). The
        pattern remaps the component's own variables to system colors —
        <code>ButtonFace</code>/<code>ButtonText</code> for buttons,
        <code>CanvasText</code> for borders, <code>LinkText</code> for links —
        and drops the shadow
        (<code>--sk-shadow-brutal-none</code>) so forced palettes do not draw a
        second boundary.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So theming stays what it is: redeclarations of <code>--sk-*</code>
      variables in one file, on a scope, per theme — with the contrast gates,
      dark mode, and media-query handling already done.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-inline-size: 65ch;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
  }
</style>
